+# Case-Insensitive Matching Is Folded in the Query, Not in the Schema
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+REQ-VET-003 narrows the veterinarian directory to one specialty. The match is on the whole name, without regard to letter case.
+
+The project already matches text case-insensitively in three places, and reaches it three different ways at the database. H2 types the pet and owner name columns `VARCHAR_IGNORECASE`. MySQL relies on its default collation. PostgreSQL lower-cases inside a functional index. The three do not agree, and the disagreement is already a recorded defect: PostgreSQL owner search is case-sensitive while the other two vendors are not.
+
+The `specialties.name` column repeats the setup with a different split. H2 types it plain `VARCHAR`, PostgreSQL types it `TEXT`, and both compare case-sensitively. Only MySQL folds case. A fourth schema-level fix would be a fourth vendor-specific expression of one rule.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Fold case in the schema, per vendor.** Retype the H2 column, add a PostgreSQL functional index, leave MySQL to its collation. Rejected: it writes the rule three times in three dialects, and the default H2 test suite cannot observe the other two. That is the exact shape that produced the PostgreSQL owner-search defect.
+2. **Fold case in the application after loading.** Read the veterinarians and compare in Java. Rejected: paging then has to happen in memory, and the page counts stop coming from the database.
+3. **Fold case in the query** (chosen). The derived query compares `UPPER(name)` on both sides. Spring Data JPA emits it; every vendor evaluates it identically.
+
+## Decision
+
+Case-insensitive matching this project performs is expressed in the query, in JPQL, not in a column type, a collation, or an index expression. The comparison is portable by construction, so one behavior holds across H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.
+
+The rule governs matching only. It does not govern uniqueness. Pet-name uniqueness stays enforced at the database, where a query-level rule cannot hold under concurrency — see [Database-Enforced Pet Name Uniqueness Within an Owner](2026-07-31-database-enforced-pet-name-uniqueness.md).
+
+The existing schema-level folding is not migrated by this decision. Retiring it is separate work against the recorded defect.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The H2-backed default test suite observes the real matching behavior. A regression fails locally rather than only on a deployed vendor.
+- An index on the raw column does not serve a folded comparison. Specialty matching therefore scans. The seeded specialty list has three rows, so the cost is not material at this size.
+- Two mechanisms for case-insensitivity now coexist: this one for matching, the schema for the pet-name constraint. The split is deliberate and stated above.
+- A later requirement that needs a folded comparison to use an index needs its own decision, on a functional index, recorded here.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [System design — Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence) — the rule in its current-state form.
+- [System design — Known Defects](../system-design.md#known-defects) — the PostgreSQL owner-search divergence this decision does not repeat.
